    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It cut off right when you were explaining the RPE part. Can you finish that explanation and show me what a sample week would look like using that RPE/RIR method instead of those weird metaphors?\n\nAlso, since you mentioned \"neuromuscular fatigue\" and \"central nervous system drive\"—can you explain what that actually feels like in a real set? Like, when i'm halfway through a heavy set of squats and i suddenly feel this sudden, weird \"glitch\" or a massive drop in power, what is actually happening biologically? I want to understand the \"why\" behind that sudden sense of failure.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies; I was interrupted mid-sentence. When I mentioned RPE 8, it means that at the point of failure, you should have had two more repetitions left in the tank if you had pushed. This objective metric replaces the need for a trainer to tell you to \"fight the fatigue guys.\"\n\nA sample week, assuming a general strength/hypertrophy goal, might involve a split focusing on compound movements with structured progression. For instance, Week 1 might see you performing 3 sets of 8 reps at RPE 7.5 on your main lift, and in Week 4, you would aim to maintain that rep count while increasing the load to achieve RPE 8.5, demonstrating overload.\n\nThe \"glitch\" or sudden drop in power during a heavy set, like squats, is often a confluence of neuromuscular fatigue and peripheral muscle failure. Biologically, this can manifest as a reduced rate of motor unit recruitment or a failure in the efferent signal transmission from the central nervous system (CNS) to the muscle fibers. Essentially, the CNS, after sustaining high levels of high-threshold motor unit firing, becomes less capable of maintaining that high-frequency output, leading to a perceived and actual drop",
      "source": "target",
